  "record_id": "batch.motif.mesopotamian-gilgamesh-old-babylonian-jastrow-clay-gutenberg-l4731-l4891",
  "source_text_path": "texts/public-domain/mesopotamian/project-gutenberg/old-babylonian-gilgamesh-jastrow-clay.md",
  "passage_locator": {
    "label": "YALE TABLET. / TRANSLITERATION. / TRANSLATION. / NOTES; lines 4731-4891",
    "start": "4731",
    "end": "4891",
    "translation": "An Old Babylonian Version of the Gilgamesh Epic",
    "notes": "Passage consists of scholarly notes discussing the name/etymology of Gilgamesh, iconographic depictions (seal cylinders), and cross-references/comparisons to Biblical material (Etana/Ethan; Eve/harimtu), plus notes on creation phrasing (Aruru; Anu; Ninib)."
  },
  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Scholarly notes: proposes and debates etymologies for the name Gilgamesh (including West Semitic origins and possible interpretation involving an axe/weapon determinative), notes iconographic difficulty distinguishing Gilgamesh and Enkidu on seal cylinders, and makes cautious comparisons to Old Testament names (Etana/Ethan; Balak) and to Genesis (Eve; the unnamed \"woman\" before the fall) in relation to the Babylonian \"harimtu\" who guides Enkidu. Also comments on creation terminology involving Aruru (creation by will/heart) and clay-moulding, and provides many cross-references to tablet lines/editions.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The notes discuss the spelling/reading of the hero's name (Gish-gi-mash) and an interpretation process that treats the first syllable as meaning \"hero.\"",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Professor Clay is reported to favor an Amoritic/West Semitic origin for the hero's name, proposing an original form like Bilga-Mash with a possible meaning \"offspring of Mash\" and also suggesting a scribal reinterpretation as \"the axe of Mash\" tied to a weapon determinative (Gish) and ideograms glossed as \"axe.\"",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The notes state that Jastrow assumes responsibility for the proposed explanation of the form and etymology of the name Gilgamesh and that differences of scholarly opinion will persist until definite evidence appears.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The notes mention an earlier scholarly equation of Etana with Ethan in the Old Testament, implying a West Semitic name.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The notes discuss seal-cylinder evidence in which Gilgamesh and Enkidu can be difficult to distinguish; some depictions show Enkidu with human legs, and others distinguish Enkidu with hoofs and different beard/hair arrangements.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The notes explicitly state that an analogy between tales does not necessarily imply dependence, but may reflect similar traditions that may have arisen independently.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The notes compare Genesis (Eve unnamed until after the fall; before that simply \"woman\") with the Babylonian tale where the woman who guides Enkidu is called harimtu (\"woman\").",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "The notes refer to Enkidu being spoken of as \"born in the field\" and discuss creation phrasing: repetition of a verb \"created\" for a \"man of Anu\" and an \"offspring of Ninib,\" with one creation described as by the will/heart of Aruru and the other as moulding out of clay.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "figures": [
    {
      "id": "fig:1",
      "name_or_label": "Gilgamesh (Gish-gi-mash)",
      "description": "The hero whose name/spelling and possible West Semitic etymology are discussed, including possible interpretation involving a weapon (axe) determinative.",
