    "summary": "Scholarly notes comment on several themes and cross-references: intoxication and gladness; Enkidu’s transition from a “primitive man” state through union with a woman despite narrative inconsistencies; Gilgamesh’s status as a “judge” likened to Shamash; increasing darkness in the hero’s wanderings; an interpretation of the Deluge as a seasonal nature-myth tied to river overflow and contrasted with creation accounts that describe drying of primeval waters (including a comparison to the Biblical creation story); an allusion to a month called the “Mission of Ishtar” tied to Ishtar’s disappearance and journey to the lower world; discussion of the Deluge survivor’s name (Zi-usudra/Ut-napishtim); and explicit discussion of a “twin-brother” motif where Gilgamesh and Enkidu are treated as twins or brothers, with mention of other paired figures as non-parallel examples.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
