    "summary": "Translator’s/annotator’s notes summarizing several mythic episodes and scholarly points: Odin’s secret exchanges for wisdom (including giving an eye to Mimir), the Gjallarhorn under Yggdrasil, the Völva addressing Odin and proceeding to prophecy, the Valkyries’ functions and origins, Baldr’s death through mistletoe and Loki’s agency, Vali’s vengeance, Loki’s binding under serpent venom with Sigyn’s aid, and brief mention of the realms/homes of gods’ enemies (giants, dwarfs, Hel’s dead).",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Heimdall possesses the Gjallarhorn, which will summon the gods to the last battle; until then it is buried under Yggdrasil.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Odin’s pledge is described as his eye, given to the water-spirit Mimir in exchange for wisdom; it is treated as a drinking-vessel from which Mimir drinks magic mead and from which he pours water on the ash Yggdrasil.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Odin’s sacrifice of his eye is connected to his search for knowledge of his final doom and is described as part of a series of disasters leading to the destruction of the gods.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The Völva addresses Odin directly, indicating she knows why he has come and what he has suffered, and her repeated question (“would you know yet more?”) is explained as asking whether he wants her to speak of his fate.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "A transitional point is described in which the Völva, rewarded by Odin for knowledge of the past, is induced to proceed with her prophecy of what is to come.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Valkyries are said to bring the bravest warriors slain in battle to Valhalla to reinforce the gods for their final struggle; they are also called Wish-Maidens as fulfillers of Odin’s wishes.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Baldr’s death is described: Frigg obtains oaths from created things not to harm Baldr except mistletoe; Loki brings mistletoe to Baldr’s blind brother Hoth and guides his throw; Baldr is slain and the gods grieve.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Vali is described as Odin’s son, begotten to avenge Baldr’s death; the day after his birth he fights and kills Hoth.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "After Baldr’s murder, Loki is bound to a rock with the bowels of his son Narfi (torn to pieces by Loki’s other son, Vali); a serpent above drips venom on Loki; Sigyn catches venom in a basin but when she empties it the venom falls again and the earth shakes; Loki remains bound until the end.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The notes mention the homes of enemies of the gods: giants, dwarfs, and the dead in the land of Hel; a river named Slith is mentioned, and swords/daggers are suggested to represent icy cold.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Frigg is identified as Odin’s wife; some scholars interpret her as a solar myth and interpret her home (Fensalir, “sea-halls”) as symbolizing the sun setting beneath the ocean horizon.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    }
