    "quote": "",
    "summary": "A sequence of maxims contrasts wise and foolish behavior: the sluggard avoids danger but cannot avoid aging; the fool reveals himself at feasts and in drink; wisdom is associated with travel and measured speech. The verses advise moderation in mead, restraint in talk, and avoiding mockery. They describe proper guest behavior and the risks of long stays, praise self-sufficiency at home, recommend carrying weapons while traveling, and counsel prudent management of wealth. They also emphasize reciprocal gift-giving among friends, warn against trusting the untrustworthy, value companionship on the road, and contrast brave/noble lives with cowardly fear and stinginess.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "A sluggard believes he will live forever if he avoids fighting, but old age will not grant peace even if weapons spare his life.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "A fool at a feast stammers or is silent; after drinking, the nature of his mind becomes visible.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "A person who has wandered widely is said to understand how minds are guided by wisdom.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Mead should not be shunned, but should be drunk in moderation; one should speak to the point or be silent.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Greed and lack of measure are mocked through images of overeating and belly-measure; herds know when to go home but the foolish do not know their limit.",
