  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "A speaking figure endures an ordeal between fires and blesses Agnar for giving a drink, promising him rule. The speaker then enumerates holy places and halls of gods and other beings (e.g., Valhalla and other divine dwellings), describes Valhalla’s martial fittings and ominous animals, names beings associated with various halls, describes the distribution of the slain, and lists cosmic features such as gates, rivers, and bridges. The passage culminates in descriptions of divine sustenance and animals, the gods’ daily riding to Yggdrasil to give judgments, and the cosmic ash Yggdrasil with three roots extending toward Hel, frost-giants, and the lands of men.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The speaker addresses fire as excessively hot and commands the flames to go, saying a mantle is burned and the fur is scorched.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The speaker says they have sat between fires for eight nights and that no one brought meat except Agnar.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The speaker hails Agnar and says that for a single drink Agnar will never receive a greater gift as reward.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "A land is called holy and said to lie near the gods and the elves together.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Multiple named halls/homes are listed for various gods and beings (e.g., Thruthheim, Ydalir, Alfheim, Valaskjolf, Sokkva-bekk, Glathsheim/Valhall, Thrymheim, Breithablik, Himinbjorg, Folkvang, Glitnir, Noatun, Vithi).",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4",
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Valhalla is described as wide, with rafters of spears, a roof of shields, benches strewn with breastplates, and a wolf hanging by the western door with an eagle hovering above.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Othin is said to choose each day the men fallen in fight; Freyja is said to choose half the dead each day, and Othin has half.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "A sacred gate (Valgrind) and holy doors are mentioned as old and tightly locked, and Valhalla is said to have five hundred and forty doors with eight hundred fighters through one door when they go to war with the wolf.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "A goat (Heithrun) bites branches of Lrath and fills a pitcher with mead; a hart (Eikthyrnir) bites branches of Lrath and a stream drops from its horns into Hvergelmir, from which all rivers run.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The gods are said to ride daily on steeds to the ash-tree Yggdrasil 'when dooms to give,' while the bridge of heaven burns in flame and sacred waters seethe.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Three roots of the ash-tree Yggdrasil are said to run three ways: beneath one lives Hel, beneath another the frost-giants, and beneath the last are the lands of men.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    }
