  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "An introductory note describes the poem’s manuscript witnesses, probable late composition, and stylistic features (including skaldic diction/kennings). The opening stanzas narrate the gods’ feasting and their demand that a being called the “mountain-dweller” provide a feast/ale; a huge kettle is needed, and Tyr identifies one owned by Hymir far east at the end of heaven. Thor and Tyr travel to Hymir’s hall, encounter Tyr’s many-headed grandmother and a fair woman who offers beer, and are hidden beneath/behind a kettle. Hymir returns from hunting; his glance breaks a beam and pillar, but the hard-hammered kettle remains intact. Hymir sees the visitors, and three steers are brought in to be boiled.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The introductory note states the poem survives complete in two manuscripts, discusses theories of compilation, and suggests a late date (possibly Icelandic, early eleventh century).",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The gods make a feast together and seek drink; they shake twigs and try blood, and they find rich fare in a hall.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "A “mountain-dweller” is addressed; the “son of Ygg” gazes into his eyes and tells him to get a feast for the gods.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "A figure called the “word-wielder” works for a giant and says he will brew much ale in a kettle; the far-famed/holy gods cannot find the kettle.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Tyr says Hymir lives east of Elivagar at the end of heaven and that Hymir owns a mighty kettle described as a vessel a mile in depth; Thor asks if they can win it, and Tyr says they can if they are cunning.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Thor and Tyr travel from Asgarth to Egil’s home and then to Hymir’s hall; Egil guards horned goats.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Tyr finds his grandmother, who has nine hundred heads; another fair, gold-adorned woman brings beer to her son and warns they will be hidden beneath the kettle because Hymir is often wrathful toward guests.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Hymir returns late from hunting, harsh and misshapen, with icicles rattling; his beard/chin is frozen.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "The visitors sit under the gable and hide behind a beam; at the giant’s glance the beam breaks and a mighty pillar falls in pieces.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Eight things fall from a ledge, but the hard-hammered kettle remains whole; then the hidden ones come out and the giant seeks his foes with his eyes.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
