    "summary": "An introductory note explains the Lokasenna as an insult-contest poem preserved in Regius, with prose notes functioning like stage directions. The prose narrative sets a feast in Ægir's hall attended by many gods. Loki kills a serving-man, is driven out, returns, speaks with the other servant Eldir, and then enters the hall to demand drink and a seat. Loki invokes a past blood-mixing pact with Othin to secure hospitality, and the gods seat him. Loki pointedly greets all except Bragi; Bragi offers gifts to prevent discord, but Loki responds with insults and challenges, while Ithun urges Bragi to restrain his speech.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
