  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "An introductory note discusses the manuscript context and stylistic links of Baldrs Draumar to Voluspo and Thrymskvitha. The poem depicts the gods meeting in council over Baldr's baleful dreams. Othin rides to Niflhel, raises a dead wise-woman from her grave with spells, and questions her. She prophesies that Baldr is being prepared for in Hel (mead brewed, shield laid over it), that Hoth will kill Baldr with a branch, and that Vali (born of Rind) will avenge Baldr by burning the slayer. The wise-woman recognizes Othin, dismisses him, and foretells a future time when Loki is loosed from bonds and destroyers come to the last strife.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The introductory note states the poem is found in the Arnamagnan Codex and preserved in late paper manuscripts under the title Vegtamskvitha, and notes it contains fourteen stanzas.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The introductory note says stanza 11 closely matches lines in Voluspo and that the poem elaborates an episode outlined there; it also reports stylistic similarity to Thrymskvitha and conjectures about common authorship.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The gods and goddesses meet in council to find why baleful dreams have come to Baldr.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Othin saddles Sleipnir and rides down to Niflhel; he meets a hound coming from hell and reaches Hel's high house.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Othin rides to the eastern door where the wise-woman's grave is, speaks magic and charms, and she rises spell-bound and speaks though dead.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The wise-woman says she was snowed on, smitten with rain, drenched with dew, and long dead.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Othin (calling himself Vegtam) asks for whom the benches and platforms are prepared with rings and gold; the wise-woman replies that mead is brewed for Baldr, a shield lies over the drink, and the gods' hope is gone.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6",
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "The wise-woman prophesies that Hoth bears a far-famed branch and will become Baldr's bane, stealing the life from Othin's son.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "The wise-woman prophesies that Rind bears Vali in Vestrsalir, and that one night old he will fight; he will not wash hands or comb hair until he brings Baldr's slayer to the flames.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The wise-woman identifies Vegtam as Othin; Othin insults her by saying she is the mother of three giants, and she tells him to ride home.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "The wise-woman says no one shall seek her more until Loki wanders loose from his bonds and the destroyers come to the last strife.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    }
  ],
