    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Editorial notes summarizing and contextualizing several stanzas: identification of named figures and genealogies; a description of the twelve Berserkers and their battle-frenzy behavior; remarks on King Jormunrek and the Volsung-Sigurth heroic cycle (including Sigurth's dragon battle); notes on Harald Battle-tooth's lineage and career; the beginning of the fragmentary Short Voluspo with references to twelve gods; and notes on Heimdall's parentage (Othin and nine mothers), the names of the mothers, and a reference to sacrificial swine's blood contributing to Heimdall's strength.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The notes describe the twelve Berserkers (sons of Arngrim and Eyfura) as wild warriors subject to battle-frenzies in which they howled like beasts, foamed at the mouth, and gnawed the iron rims of their shields; during these fits they were said to be proof against steel or fire, but afterward they were weak.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The notes connect Ottar's descent to the Volsung–Sigurth–Jormunrek–Gjuki genealogy and state that Sigurth's battle with the dragon Fafnir is treated elsewhere.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Jormunrek is identified with the historical Ermanarich and is said (in Norse tradition) to have slain his own sons; he is also said in the saga tradition to have married Svanhild, Sigurth's daughter.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "A list of twelve gods (in addition to Othin) is attributed to Snorri, and Loki is described as of doubtful divinity in that list.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Heimdall is described (per Snorri) as the son of Othin and nine sisters; the notes add that the names of these nine giantess mothers are often taken to denote attributes of the sea, and a suggested rendering of the names is provided.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The notes state that 'swine's blood' added to Heimdall's strength is derived from sacrifice, in addition to strength drawn from earth and sea.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The notes recount Harald Battle-tooth's lineage (Hrrek, Auth, Ivar, Rathbarth, Randver, Sigurth Ring) and state that his death occurred on the Bravalla-field at the hands of his nephew Sigurth Ring.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    }
