      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Fjolsvith challenges Svipdag, calls him friendless, and tells him to leave and not enter the house.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Fjolsvith names himself and says he is wise but miserly with meat; he declares Svipdag will never enter within the house.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Svipdag says he seeks the sight of his love and asserts the gates of the golden hall are bright and that he will enjoy a home there.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Svipdag gives his name and lineage: he calls himself Vindkald, Varkald’s son, and says Fjolkald was Varkald’s father.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Fjolsvith says Mengloth rules the rich hall; her mother bore her to the son of Svafrthorin.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The gate is called Thrymgjol; it was made by three, the sons of Solblindi; it holds fast whoever lifts the latch.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "The house is called Gastropnir; Fjolsvith says he made it from the limbs of Leirbrimir and braced it to stand as long as the world lasts.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "A tree named Mimameith casts its limbs over every land; no one knows what root runs beneath it; fire nor iron can fell it.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Women sick with child seek the tree’s fruit to carry to the flames; then what was hidden within comes out.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "A cock named Vithofnir sits on the highest bough and glitters with gold, shining like lightning on the tree’s limbs; it troubles Surt and Sinmora.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Two hounds, Gif and Geri, are fierce before the house; their might will grow until the gods are doomed to death.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "The hounds do not sleep together: one sleeps at night and the other by day, so no man may enter ever.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "The only meat that can be given to the hounds so one may leap within while they eat is two wing-joints from Vithofnir’s body.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "A weapon called Lvatein was made by Lopt with runes by the doors of death; it lies in Lgjarn’s chest with Sinmora, fastened by nine locks.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:15"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "To take the weapon and return, a person must carry something few can win to give to the ‘goddess of gold’ (Sinmora).",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:16"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "The treasure to give Sinmora is a bright sickle carried in the wallet, found among Vithofnir’s feathers; giving it to Sinmora will result in the weapon being granted.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:17"
      ]
    }
