  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Editorial prose notes discuss characters, setting, manuscript issues, and interpretive points for Völundarkviða. They identify Nithuth as a king (here said to be of Sweden), Vlund/Völund as an elf in the poem (though later prose calls him a king of the Finns), and Bothvild as Vlund’s victim in the latter part of the poem. The notes discuss the swan-maidens episode and the debated identification of the swan-maidens as Valkyries, mention possible lost lines describing the theft of swan-garments, explain that a ring stolen by Nithuth’s men is given to Bothvild and leads to her undoing, and state that Nithuth comes with his men to capture Vlund and accuse him of stealing treasure. The notes also describe various mythical place-names (e.g., Myrkwood, Ulfdalir, Ulfsjar, Valland) and cross-reference other Eddic poems and sagas for parallels or name-forms.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
