  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "At a coastal landing and aboard a longship, Sigrun questions Helgi about recent fighting and blood on his armor; Helgi recounts killing enemies and feeding eagles with the sword. A prose narrative introduces King Granmar and his sons, Sigrun's unwanted betrothal to Hothbrodd, and Sigrun's journey with valkyries to find Helgi, whom she embraces and chooses despite fear of kin-anger. Helgi raises a sea-host; a storm with lightning strikes the ship, nine valkyries appear in the air (with Sigrun recognized among them), and the storm abates. A great battle follows in which Granmar's sons and allies fall; Dag is spared and swears loyalty. Sigrun rejects the dying Hothbrodd; Helgi laments that Bragi and Hogni (Sigrun's kin) fell by his hand, and both speak of fate and grief. A further exchange identifies Helgi as fleet-leader with a golden flag and foretells swords proving worth at Frekastein.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "A ship is by a steep shore; the speakers say they have a home in Hlesey and are waiting for a fair wind at Brunavagar to travel eastward.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Sigrun asks Helgi where he has fought, why his mail-coat is spattered with blood, and why he is eating uncooked food while wearing a helmet.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Helgi says he captured bears in Bragalund and fed eagles with the sword; he explains their bloody shirts and that little of their food is cooked with fire.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Sigrun states that Hunding the king was bent down before Helgi and that Helgi avenged his kin; she describes blood flowing on the sword-blade.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Sigrun says she was not far away when the monarch was slain and claims she has seen Helgi on a longship amid raging icy waves; Helgi is said to be known to Hogni's daughter (Sigrun).",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "A prose narrative names King Granmar of Svarin's hill and his sons, including Hothbrodd; Hothbrodd gains a promise to marry Sigrun, Hogni's daughter.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "When Sigrun hears of the promise, she rides with the Valkyries over air and sea to seek Helgi; Helgi is at Logafjoll, weary from fighting Hunding's sons, and sits under the eagle-stone; Sigrun finds him, embraces him, kisses him, and tells her news.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "In verse, Sigrun takes Helgi's hand, greets and kisses him; she says she loved Sigmund's son before seeing him and that she was mated to Hothbrodd at a meeting but would rather have another hero, fearing her kin's wrath for breaking her father's wish.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Helgi tells Sigrun not to fear Hogni's anger or her kinsmen's wrath and says she will live with him and he will not fear her kindred.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Helgi assembles a great sea-host and sails to Frekastein; at sea they meet a dangerous storm with lightning and bolts striking the ship; nine Valkyries are seen riding in the air and Sigrun is recognized among them; the storm abates and they reach land safely.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Granmar's sons sit on a mountain watching the ships approach; Gothmund rides to a promontory near the harbor for news and speaks about the king who captains the fleet; Sinfjotli answers him.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Granmar's sons summon an army; many kings come, including Hogni (Sigrun's father) and his sons Bragi and Dag; a great battle occurs in which all Granmar's sons and their allies are slain; Dag is spared and swears loyalty to the Volsungs.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Sigrun goes among the dead, finds Hothbrodd dying, and says she will never be held in his arms; she says Granmar's sons have grown cold and that gray giant-steeds gorge on corpses.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "Helgi tells Sigrun that not all her fortune is fair and blames the Norns; he says Bragi and Hogni fell beneath his hand at Frekastein and describes other deaths and fighters continuing when the head had fallen; he says many of Sigrun's race lie slain and that she has won nothing because it was her fate to bring brave men to battle.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "Sigrun weeps; Helgi tells her not to grieve because the battle is gained and the fighter cannot shun fate; Sigrun says she would call the slaughtered to life if she could be safe on Helgi's breast.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:15"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "Gothmund asks what great hero guides the ships, noting a golden flag on the stem and a red battle-light around the fighters; Sinfjotli replies that Helgi is in the midst of the fleet and says he rules over the realm of fishes; Gothmund calls for swords at Frekastein to prove worth and for vengeance if they were once worsted.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:16"
      ]
    }
