  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "An editor/annotator's prose notes discuss manuscript issues, likely interpolations, cross-references and borrowing between Helgi poems, and identify (sometimes conjecturally) figures and places. The notes mention wolves as the usual steeds of giantesses, a disputed \"maid\" term that may mean Valkyrie, Norns, a quarrel scene between Gothmund and Sinfjotli signaled by a war-banner, and a new section concerning Helgi's death at the hands of Dag (Sigrun's brother), with blame attributed to Othin and mention of Othin's victory-making spear. Various rivers, mountains, shrines, and mythical places are noted, along with a late tradition of Helgi sharing Othin's rule in Valhall.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The annotator is said to have drawn most information from Helgakvitha Hundingsbana I, which is explicitly mentioned and quoted; the only new features noted are the names of Hogni's sons, Bragi and Dag (with Dag's naming described as puzzling).",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Sevafjoll is glossed as \"Wet Mountain\" and is said to be mentioned only in this poem.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "\"Giant-steeds\" are identified as wolves, described as the usual steeds of giantesses.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "A word translated as \"maid\" is described as doubtful and possibly meaning specifically a Valkyrie or simply \"wise\"; Norns are referenced; in a later stanza Dag is said to blame Othin for a murder.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "A stanza is characterized as likely an interpolation; place names Hlebjorg (\"Sea-Mountain\") and Styrkleifar (\"Battle-Cliffs\") are noted; a note connects a stanza to Saxo Grammaticus' story of Starkath, including the detail that a severed head bit the ground in anger.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "A dispute between Gothmund and Sinfjotli is introduced, and its longer form appears in Helgakvitha Hundingsbana I; the version here is described as likely older and possibly the source for borrowing in the other poem; a banner (flag) serves as the signal for war instead of a red shield; \"battle-light\" is suggested to be the northern lights.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "A new section is said to deal with Helgi's death at the hands of Dag (Sigrun's brother); the annotator introduces the name Dag and references Othin's spear as a victory-making weapon; Fjoturlund (\"Fetter-Wood\") is mentioned.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Leipt is identified as a river; Uth is described as a daughter of the sea-god gir, and mention is made of her sacred stone; Dag's life is said to have been spared because he swore loyalty to Helgi.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Vandilsve (\"Vandils Shrine\") and Vigdalir (\"Battle-Dale\") are described as purely mythical places.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "A prose note mentions Valhall and states there is no indication where the annotator got the notion of Helgi sharing Othin's rule; the idea is described as likely a late development of tradition.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    }
