  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "An introductory note describes how the Sigurth/Sigurðr legend combines multiple historical traditions (Burgundians vs. Huns, Attila/Atli, Ermanarich/Jormunrek) and how it changes in Scandinavia (names altered; links to Othin and to Fafnir’s treasure; Brynhild reimagined as a Valkyrie on a flame-girt rock, though inconsistently). The poem then begins: Sigurth rides alone to his uncle Gripir’s hall, speaks with the man Geitir, is welcomed by Gripir, asks for a prophecy about his life, and is told he will be the mightiest of men, will avenge his father and Eylimi by killing Hunding’s sons, and will fight a fiery dragon at Gnitaheith, slaying Regin and Fafnir.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The passage states that in 437 the Burgundians under king Gundicarius were practically annihilated by the Huns, and that this battle became a major Rhineland tradition with Attila introduced into it.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "It says Attila’s death (453) was added to the tradition, and in the sixth century stories of Sigurth were linked to the Burgundian slaughter; Gundicarius becomes Gunther/Gunnar and Attila becomes Etzel/Atli.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "It says traditions about Ermanarich (d. c. 376) were later added; he is introduced as husband of Sigurth’s daughter Svanhild, and as Jormunrek he appears in Eddic poems.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The passage claims that after coming to Norway the story changed: names received Scandinavian forms (e.g., Kriemhild becomes Guthrun), new secondary figures were introduced, and Northern local color was added.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "It states the earlier part of the story was linked with Northern mythology: the Volsungs are made descendants of Othin, and the gods are closely concerned with Fafnir’s treasure.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "It states the Norse storytellers made Brynhild a Valkyrie sleeping on a flame-girt rock, but she also remains (to an extent) an entirely human daughter of Buthli whom Sigurth woos for Gunnar, creating confusion with mixed names.",
