    "summary": "During a storm at sea, Sigurth and Regin encounter a man who gives several names and boards their ship, after which the storm subsides. Sigurth asks for the best signs for battle, and the man lists omens and practical cautions. Sigurth later fights Lyngvi (son of Hunding) and Lyngvi and his brothers are killed. Regin then describes the \"bloody eagle\" carved on the back of Sigmund's killer and urges Sigurth onward to fight Fafnir.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "A speaker asks who rides on Rvil's steeds over towering waves and wild waters, describing the \"sail-horses\" as dripping with sweat and the sea-steeds unable to withstand the gale.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Regin states that Sigurth and Regin are on the \"sea-trees\" and that the storm wind is driving them toward death as waves crash on the forward deck and the \"roller-steeds\" sink.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The man identifies himself with multiple names (including Hnikar, the Man from the Mountain, Feng, and Fjolnir) and says he will fare with them.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "They sail to land; the man goes aboard their ship; the storm subsides.",
