  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Editorial notes discuss gaps and interpolations in Sigrdrifumol and related stanzas about Brynhild and Sigurth. The notes include a prose paraphrase from the Volsungasaga describing mutual desire and the binding of oaths, identify a section of numbered counsels (attributed to Brynhild) resembling Loddfafnismol/Hovamol, gloss an epithet meaning “oath-breaker,” describe an interpolated maxim about silence and retaliatory killing, and explain advice preferring death in battle over being burned in a house (with Njalssaga cited as an example). Manuscript loss and later additions are also described.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The notes state that only stanzas 20–21 remain from an older dialogue between Brynhild and Sigurth, with intervening stanzas lost; Brynhild is said to have warned Sigurth about perils if he swears loyalty to her.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "A prose paraphrase in the Volsungasaga is cited: Sigurth declares Brynhild the wisest and swears to have her; she replies she would rather have him than choose among all men; they bind this with oaths.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The notes say a section beginning with a stanza starts a list of numbered counsels attributed to Brynhild and closely resembling Loddfafnismol (Hovamol 111–138), while also stating the attribution to Brynhild is probably not original.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The phrase “Wolf of his word” is glossed as meaning an oath-destroyer or oath-breaker.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "One interpolated passage is summarized as warning that long silence may be taken for cowardice; if falsely taunted due to silence, one should not argue but kill the taunter the next morning as proof he is a liar.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "A counsel is explained as meaning it is better to go forth to battle than to stay at home and be burned to death; house-burning is noted as a way Norse warriors sometimes died, with Njalssaga mentioned as a famous example.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The notes describe manuscript loss in the Codex Regius and later paper-manuscript additions (including stanzas 30–37), and state the Volsungasaga paraphrases the additional stanzas.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    }
