    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Guthrun lies grieving and weeps for Sigurth; her sister Gollrond speaks of Guthrun’s great love and unhappiness. Guthrun praises Sigurth and declares herself diminished now that he is dead, blaming the sons of Gjuki and reminding Gunnar of oaths sworn with Sigurth, warning that gold and rings will bring death. Brynhild and Gollrond exchange hostile words; Brynhild blames Atli and recalls seeing a fiery ‘snake’s bed’ around the hero, and she is described as burning with fire and breathing venom upon seeing Sigurth’s wounds. In prose aftermath, Guthrun departs into the wilderness and goes to Denmark for years; Brynhild will not live after Sigurth, has thralls and serving-women killed, and then kills herself with a sword.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
