    "summary": "An introductory note discusses the placement, length, composition date, and probable origin of the poem Sigurtharkvitha en skamma, including its relation to other Eddic materials and prose/verse traditions. The excerpt then presents stanzas in which Sigurth seeks Gjuki and swears oaths with Gjuki's sons; he is given Guthrun in marriage with jewels; the group goes to woo Brynhild; a scene describes a naked sword placed between a man and a maiden and the maiden being given to Gjuki's sons. Brynhild laments her situation, expresses desire for Sigurth despite death, blames fate (Norns), and wanders grieving on glacier ice at evening. She threatens Gunnar with loss and departure unless he dares Sigurth's death, and speaks of killing a son to reduce vengeance-payment. Gunnar grieves, considers killing Sigurth though he loves him, and calls Hogni for counsel.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
