  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "An introductory note situates the poem \"Helreith Brynhildar\" in manuscript context and discusses its composition and dating. A prose bridge and stanzas then depict Brynhild’s post-mortem wagon-journey on the Hel-way: she passes a giantess’s rock-walled dwelling and trades accusations and defenses. Brynhild recounts her earlier identity (named Hild, associated with swan-robes), actions in battle that angered Othin, and the punishment of an enchanted sleep with shields and a burning barrier around her hall to be broken by a fearless hero who brings Fafnir’s guarded gold. She describes sleeping beside Sigurth without touching, then being reproached by Guthrun and learning of betrayal in marriage; she ends by asserting that she and Sigurth will live together.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The introductory note states the poem follows the short Sigurth lay in the Codex Regius and is linked by a brief prose note; it says the poem (except stanza 6) is quoted in the Nornageststhattr and comments on dramatic unity and interpolation claims.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The introductory note states the poem is generally dated as late as the eleventh century and that the concluding stanza shows almost unmistakable Christian influence; it also notes Brynhild is presented as both Valkyrie and swan-maiden and that the poem is essentially a Norse creation with few details of the German cycle.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "After Brynhild’s death two bale-fires are made: one for Sigurth (burned first) and one for Brynhild; Brynhild is burned on a wagon covered with a rich cloth, and she goes in the wagon on the Hel-way.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Brynhild passes a house where a giantess dwells; the giantess addresses Brynhild and tells her not to go further, saying it would be more fitting to stay weaving than to follow another’s husband.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The giantess calls Brynhild \"Gold-goddess\" and says Brynhild has washed heroes’ blood from her hands.",
