    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Editorial notes discuss variant traditions and textual issues around Brynhild and Sigurth (pyre(s), meetings, duration of nights, a sword placed between them), identify mythic place-names and kennings (e.g., fire), describe a swan-maiden/Valkyrie motif involving stolen swan-garments, and remark that a future-life reunion theme appears Christian-influenced.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The notes state that the prose follows directly after the last stanza of Sigurtharkvitha en skamma, and mention a contradiction between 'two bale-fires' and another account where Sigurth and Brynhild are burned on the same pyre.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The notes explain that 'Valland' is used elsewhere for mythical places and may not be a proper name here; 'Gold-goddess' is glossed as a poetic circumlocution for 'woman.'",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The notes report manuscript/edition variation in the placement of two lines, suggest lines may be lost, and state that it has often been argued that stanzas 6–10 (in whole or part) come originally from Sigrdrifumol; 'Hild' is identified as a Valkyrie name and 'Hlymdalir' is explained as a mythical battle-place home of Valkyries.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The notes state that swan-maidens are identified with Valkyries, and that men could get them in their power by stealing their swan-garments; this is compared to Vlundarkvitha where the same thing happens. The notes also discuss uncertainty about 'the monarch' and a variant reading about 'eight' versus 'sisters of Atli.'",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The notes describe Hjalmgunnar as a king of the Goths (with the phrase said to mean little) and reference variant manuscript readings (e.g., 'brother of the giantess' instead of 'leader of Goths').",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The notes identify 'Skatalund' as a mythical name and note that elsewhere the place where Brynhild lay is called Hindarfjoll.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The notes gloss 'Branches foe' as 'fire' and mention that the 'treasure' should be compared with Fafnismol.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "The notes call one stanza an interpolation reflecting a version where Sigurth meets Brynhild at the home of her brother-in-law and foster-father Heimir; they identify Grani as Sigurth's horse and note uncertainty about Sigurth appearing 'in this capacity' among the Danes, suggesting a possible relic of the Helgi tradition.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "The notes contrast 'eight nights' with another tradition of 'three nights' and describe a confusion of traditions; they state that in one version Sigurth was not bound by oaths and the union was completed, while in another the episode includes a sword laid between the two.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The notes say that a concluding idea that Sigurth and Brynhild will be together in some future life is out of keeping with Norse pagan traditions and indicates Christian influence.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
