  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Guthrun recalls weaving scenes of warriors and ships, then describes Grimhild’s efforts to settle compensation and arrange Guthrun’s remarriage through gifts and a brewed draught with magical ingredients and runes that makes Guthrun forget her grief. Guthrun refuses Atli, foresees harm to Gunnar and Hogni, but is pressured and later journeys to Atli’s hall. Atli recounts terrifying dreams (Guthrun stabbing him; blood-red uprooted plants to be eaten; hawks flying to an evil house; eating hearts with honey, soaked in blood), and Guthrun interprets one dream as foretelling pain and her intention to heal him despite hatred.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Guthrun describes weaving (broidering) tapestries depicting southern halls, Danish swans, armed hosts, and warriors’ deeds.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "A scene of \"Sigmund's ship\" sailing by land is included in the woven imagery; the ship has a golden figurehead and beaks; warriors faring are named (Sigar and Siggeir) going south to Fjon.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Grimhild casts aside her needlework, calls her sons, and asks who will make amends for their sister’s son or repay a wife for a husband killed; Gunnar and Hogni are ready to give gold as amends.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Princes/men come described as \"long-beard men\" in red mantles with mail-coats, helms, and swords; each tries to comfort Guthrun with gifts and speech, but she does not trust them.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Grimhild gives Guthrun a bitter, cold draught that causes her to forget her cares; the mixture includes \"magic earth,\" ice-cold sea, and swine’s blood; the cup contains runes Guthrun cannot read and additional items (a heather-fish, an uncut ear, and entrails of beasts).",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The brewed beer is described as containing \"blossoms of trees,\" burned acorns, \"dew of the hearth,\" holy entrails, and swine liver, said to allay grief.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "After receiving the draught in the hall, Guthrun forgets her husband’s slaying; three kings kneel before Grimhild speaks and offers Guthrun wealth, rings, halls, and hangings, as well as Hunnish women skilled in weaving and the wealth of Buthli, positioning Guthrun as Atli’s wife.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Guthrun refuses to take a husband and specifically refuses being wife of Brynhild’s brother and Buthli’s son; Grimhild urges her not to seek vengeance on men and promises happiness as if Sigurth and Sigmund still lived if she bears sons.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Guthrun states she cannot find gladness since raven and wolf lapped Sigurth’s heart-blood; Grimhild insists the ruler she found is nobly born and Guthrun must take him or remain husbandless.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Guthrun warns that if sent to the baneful race, Atli’s wrath will fall first on Gunnar and he will tear the heart from Hogni’s breast; Grimhild weeps at the foretold fate and gives Guthrun lands (Vinbjorg and Valbjorg).",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Guthrun says she must do as the kings asked, wed against her will for her kinsmen, had no joy with her husband, and her sons were not saved by her brothers’ fate; she says she could not rest until she robbed the bold warrior (battle-maker) of life (text partially lacunose).",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "A journey follows: heroes ride horseback, foreign women travel in wagons; they travel a week through cold lands, a second week over waves, and a third through lands lacking water; warders on lofty walls open gates and they ride in.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Atli wakes Guthrun; he says the Norns woke him from sleep with visions of terror, and he reports dreams: Guthrun pierced him with a poisoned blade; drooping garden plants were pulled up by the roots, red with blood, brought to him to eat; his hawks flew to an evil house; he ate their hearts with honey, soaked in blood, causing sorrow.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "Guthrun responds that a dream of steel will be followed by fire and woman’s wrath; she says she will burn out a baneful sore from Atli and tend and heal him though he is hated.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:14"
      ]
    }
