    "summary": "An introductory note situates the poem within the Sigurth–Atli legend cycle and comments on its likely date, sources, and relationship to German material (including a close parallel to the Nibelungenlied). A prose setup explains that Borgny cannot give birth until Oddrun (Atli's sister and Gunnar's beloved) comes. In the poem, Oddrun rides to Heithrek's hall, learns Borgny is in pain after a secret relationship with Vilmund, and uses magic chants to help her deliver a boy and a girl. Borgny thanks the gods; Oddrun says she acts from an old oath. The women argue about past slights connected to Gunnar. Oddrun then begins recounting her earlier life, her father's final instructions about a dowry and marriage, and describes Brynhild as a powerful 'wish-maid'; she also mentions a battle in which a city was broken and Brynhild recognized evil wiles.",
