  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "An introductory note discusses the two Atli poems in the Codex Regius (Atlakvitha and Atlamol), their manuscript attribution to Greenland, likely dating and Christian influence, metrical characteristics and possible reuse of older material, and their narrative basis (sons of Gjuki visit Atli’s court, are killed, and their sister Guthrun—Atli’s wife—takes revenge by killing Atli’s sons, killing Atli, and burning the hall). It also notes the story’s South-Germanic (Attila/Burgundian) origins and limited distinctively Norse narrative content. The passage ends with a prose lead-in and the first stanza in which Atli sends a rider, Knefrth, to Gunnar at Gjuki’s home.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Two Atli poems (Atlakvitha and Atlamol) are stated to be in the Codex Regius and are not preserved or quoted in any other old manuscript; they were used extensively by the compilers of the Volsungasaga.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The manuscript superscription includes the word “Greenland” for each poem; the note says this indicates that around 1300 there was a tradition that the poems originated in the Greenland settlement, while also noting the scribe was not infallible.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Both poems are said to treat the same main story material: the sons of Gjuki visit Atli’s court, die there, and their sister Guthrun (Atli’s wife) later takes revenge on Atli.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The note states Guthrun’s revenge includes killing Atli’s sons, killing Atli, and burning the hall with his whole company.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2",
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The note proposes both poems likely date to the eleventh century and describes the poets as apparently Christian, citing (a) an Atlamol statement about dying and going into “another light” and (b) mythological names used with an apparent lack of understanding in the Atlakvitha.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The note claims the narrative material is not distinctively Norse and represents the southern blending of the Attila and Burgundian legends; it says the Atlakvitha even uses the word “Burgundians,” and that the material goes back to South-Germanic origins.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The note describes the Atlakvitha as metrically irregular/chaotic and suggests it may be a Malahattr reworking of an older Fornyrthislag Atli poem; it similarly suggests the Atlamol used older material.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "In the prose lead-in to the poem, Guthrun is identified as Gjuki’s daughter and is said to have avenged her brothers by killing Atli’s sons, then Atli, and burning the hall with his company.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
