  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "An editorial introductory note describes the origin, date, and qualities of the Atlamol (Atli lay) and summarizes its plot in relation to the older Atlakvitha and later prose (Volsungasaga). The passage then begins the poem: Atli sends a message inviting the sons of Gjuki; a wise woman makes warning runes that are falsified by Vingi; emissaries arrive; Gunnar and Hogni host them with ale and fire; wives Kostbera and Glaumvor welcome them; after feasting, Kostbera tries to read the runes by firelight and is troubled; she has a dream and speaks to Hogni.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The introductory note states the poem's manuscript superscription is generally accepted as correct and that the poem was composed in Greenland; it cites a specific reference to polar bears in stanza 17 as supporting evidence for Greenland origin.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The introductory note dates the poem nearer 1100 than 1050 and says it has come down in fairly good shape though with possible gaps and suspected minor interpolations/omissions.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The introductory note summarizes the poem's substance: Atli sends a message to the sons of Gjuki; they journey to Atli's home; Hogni and Gunnar are slain; Guthrun is bitter over her brothers' deaths; she takes bloody revenge on Atli.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The introductory note states the Atlamol shares its basic outline with the Atlakvitha but differs in additions: sea voyages replace land journeys; Atli is reproached for weakness at the Thing/council rather than for cowardice in battle; added characters and episodes aim to appeal to Greenland settlers.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The introductory note characterizes the story as a Frankish blending of legends about the slaughter of the Burgundians and the death of Attila, and says the poem shows how a story brought north from South Germanic lands could be adapted to 11th-century hearers without changing the basic narrative.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The introductory note says the poet likely knew older poems on the subject (possibly the Atlakvitha), doubts some details (e.g., Kostbera and Glaumvor) existed in earlier tradition, and notes that a son of Hogni who aids in killing Atli appears in other late versions under another name.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The introductory note says the poem is in Malahattr meter throughout, with more regular verse than the Atlakvitha, and that the compilers of the Volsungasaga knew it in much the present form and paraphrased it with fidelity.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "In the opening stanzas, men gather in counsel, plot in secret, and deceive the trust of Gjuki's sons.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Atli sends a message that his kinsmen should seek him; the stanza describes this as ill counsel for Atli and as shaping his own sorrow.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "A wise woman perceives secret speech; she fashions runes, but Vingi makes them false before delivering them.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Warriors sent by Atli travel and come to Limafjord, to the home of the kings.",
