  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "An introductory note outlines how the Ermanarich (Jormunrek) cruelty-and-vengeance story (attested in Jordanes) blended with the Sigurth/Nibelung cycle in Norse tradition. A prose synopsis recounts Guthrun’s attempted self-drowning after killing Atli, her being carried by waves to King Jonak and bearing sons, and the death of her daughter Svanhild under horses after Bikki’s counsel leads to Randver’s hanging. The opening stanzas depict Guthrun inciting her sons to avenge Svanhild, and Hamther answering with a reproach that recalls Sigurth’s killing and Guthrun’s blood-stained bedcovers.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The note states that the poems Guthrunarhvot and Hamthesmol belong to a narrative cycle only loosely connected to Sigurth, the Burgundians, and Atli.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The note reports that Jordanes tells of Sunilda/Sanielh being torn apart by wild horses on Ermanarich’s orders, and that her brothers Sarus and Ammius wounded Ermanarich in attempted vengeance.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The note describes a process of legend-blending that connects the Ermanarich story with the Nibelung/Sigurth story by making Sunilda (Svanhild) the daughter of Sigurth and Guthrun and making her brothers correspond to Sorli and Hamther as sons of Guthrun and Jonak.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "In the prose synopsis, Guthrun goes to the sea after slaying Atli, attempts to drown, but cannot sink; waves carry her across a fjord to King Jonak’s land, where Jonak takes her as wife; their sons are Sorli, Erp, and Hamther; Svanhild, Sigurth’s daughter, is brought up and married to Jormunrek.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Bikki counsels that Randver (the king’s son) should have Svanhild; Bikki tells this to the king; the king has Randver hanged and Svanhild trodden to death under horses’ feet; Guthrun then speaks with her sons.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "In the opening stanzas, Guthrun incites her sons to fight with grim words and asks why they sit idle, invoking the fact that Jormunrek trampled their young sister beneath horses’ hoofs.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Guthrun compares her sons unfavorably to Gunnar and Hogni, saying they would quickly take vengeance if they were as brave as her brothers of old or as hard-hearted as the Hunnish kings.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Hamther responds that Guthrun did not love Hogni’s deed when Sigurth was awakened from sleep; he says her white bedcovers were red with the blood of her husband, drenched with gore from his heart.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    }
  ],
