  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Guthrun arms her sons and urges vengeance for the deaths in her family, then sits by the gate weeping and recounts a sequence of griefs: husbands, Sigurth's death, being given to Atli, killing the Hniflungs, trying to drown herself but surviving, bearing sons by Jonak, losing her beloved daughter Svanhild, and the deaths/torments of other kin. She calls on Sigurth with a remembered pledge to meet across hell and earth, and asks nobles to build a high oak pyre so fire may burn away her sorrow.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Guthrun is said to have had bloody revenge for her brothers when she caused or permitted her sons to be slain; the speaker says they could have together avenged their sisters by killing Jormunrek.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The speaker demands the gear of the Hunnish kings and says Guthrun has whetted them for sword-battle.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Laughing, Guthrun goes to her chamber, takes helms and broad mail-coats from cupboards, brings them to her sons, and the heroes leap onto their horses.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Hamther says he will not come home again to see his mother, and predicts Guthrun will drink a single death-draught for Svanhild and for her sons.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Weeping, Guthrun (Gjukis daughter) sits sadly before the gate and tells of her many griefs.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Guthrun says she knew three home-fires/hearths and was brought home by three husbands; she says only Sigurth was dear, and that her brothers brought him to his death.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Guthrun says she suffered when princes gave her to Atli.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Guthrun says she summoned brave boys to secret speech and could not win requital until she hewed off the heads of the Hniflungs.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Guthrun says she went to the sea to escape the wrath of the Norns, but lofty billows bore her undrowned until she came to land and had to live longer.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Guthrun says she came a third time to the bed of a king of the folk and bore boys to be his heirs, the sons of Jonak.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Guthrun says handmaidens sat around Svanhild, dearest of her children, and describes Svanhild as fair like a ray of the sun; she gave her gold and bright garments before letting her go to the Gothic folk.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Guthrun says one of her hardest woes was when Svanhild's fair tresses were trodden in the mire by the hoofs of wild horses.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Guthrun says Sigurth was killed on his couch, and says it was grim when bright-hued crawling snakes crept to Gunnar's heart; she also mentions the cutting of a brave king's heart from his living breast.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "Guthrun calls to Sigurth to bridle his black steed and let his swift horse run to her, saying no son or daughter remains to give her gifts.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "Guthrun recalls that she and Sigurth once said he would come mightily to her from hell and she from earth to him.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "Guthrun asks jarls to pile up the highest oak pyre a hero ever had, and asks that the fire burn her grief-filled breast and sore-pressed heart until her sorrows melt; she ends by wishing nobles and women less sorrow since the lament is told.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    }
