  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "An introductory note describes the poem Hamthesmol as poorly preserved and likely a later patchwork. The poem’s narrative (stanzas 1–18) presents Guthrun urging her sons Hamther and Sorli toward vengeance for their sister Svanhild, who was killed by Jormunrek by being trampled by horses. The sons argue with Guthrun about earlier killings in the family. They set out on horseback for vengeance, meet their wise half-brother Erp, and after insulting him, they kill him with swords, thereby diminishing their own strength.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The introductory note characterizes Hamthesmol as the worst preserved poem in the Codex Regius and describes the extant form as a patchwork of fragments and mixed meters, likely assembled by an eleventh-century narrator.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Guthrun (daughter of Gjuki) is said to have whetted her young sons to seek vengeance for Svanhild.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Svanhild is said to have been trampled by horses at Jormunrek’s command (or by Jormunrek), with the steeds described as belonging to the Goths.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Guthrun describes herself as alone of her kin and uses tree imagery (aspen, fir without boughs, leaves lost) to express loneliness and loss.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Hamther reproaches Guthrun about her reaction to Sigurth’s killing and mentions Gunnar’s responsibility; blood and gore are described as on bed-covers.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Hamther also references striking at Atli by slaying Erp and killing Eitil, with a general statement about a sword that kills another but does not strike oneself.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Sorli states he will not quarrel with his mother and questions what Guthrun asks that will bring her no tears.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Sorli predicts Guthrun will weep for them as well and that they are doomed on their steeds and will die far from home.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Guthrun gives counsel and foretells that two heroes alone will bind or slay two hundred Goths in a lofty-walled burg.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Hamther and Sorli depart from the courtyard, travel swiftly over a wet mountain on Hunnish steeds, and go to take vengeance for death.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "On the way the brothers meet a wise man identified as their half-brother; they call him a weakling and ask what help they can have from him.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Erp replies that he can aid them as one foot helps the other or as one hand helps another, and he is called a bastard; shortly thereafter, the brothers draw swords and kill the young fighter.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11",
        "ev:12"
      ]
    }
  ],
