  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Zohak’s keeping of two devouring serpents spreads terror and helps him win Persian nobles and Arab troops against King Jemshid. Jemshid is defeated, becomes a wandering exile, and eventually reaches Zabulistan. There, King Greng’s exceptionally capable daughter—beautiful, warlike, and permitted to choose her spouse by love—has a nurse from Kabul skilled in sorcery and prophecy. The nurse foretells that the daughter will marry Jemshid and bear him a beautiful son. When Jemshid arrives in spring, he rests at Greng’s garden gate under a tree and asks for wine; the daughter learns of him, goes to the gate, invites him into the garden, declares her autonomy, and finally takes his hand and leads him along the garden walks as he grows enamoured.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Zohak keeps near him two devouring serpents, causing widespread alarm; Persian nobles abandon allegiance to Jemshid and ally with Zohak and Arab troops.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Jemshid resists for a time but is defeated, loses the imperial crown, and becomes a wanderer; Zohak is described as a tyrant/usurper who erases Jemshid’s name from royal acts.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Zohak sends spies to find and seize Jemshid, but they do not succeed; Jemshid takes refuge in Zabulistan after many misfortunes.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The king of Zabulistan is named Greng; he has a daughter of extreme beauty who is also mentally gifted and familiar with warlike exercises.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Greng’s daughter dresses in the warlike habiliments of a man and combats heroes; at fifteen, she forces Minuchihr (then attacking her father) to relinquish his pretensions and submit, saving her father’s realm.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Greng refuses other suitors and agrees his daughter may marry the sovereign she spontaneously loves; the daughter states her father permits her to do as she pleases and that her heart is her own.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The daughter’s nurse is a Kabul woman skilled in magic and sorcery, including drawing fire from water and killing a dragon (sealing its eyes in death); she knows the moon and seven stars and claims knowledge of invisible things.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "The nurse prophesies—based on heavenly motions—that the daughter will marry King Jemshid and bear him a beautiful son; Greng and the daughter rejoice and refuse other suitors.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Jemshid arrives at Zabul in spring (roses in bloom), is stopped at the garden, sits by the garden door under a tree, and asks a slave-girl for wine to ease fatigue and affliction; the slave-girl reports him to the princess.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The princess goes to the gate, invites the stranger into the garden for wine (and offers music), declares her status as Greng’s only child, and then takes Jemshid by the hand and leads him along the garden walks as he becomes enamoured.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    }
