  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "A princess hosts an unknown youth (later recognized as King Jemshid) at a garden fountain with carpets, foot-washing, music, and wine. She suspects he is a king and considers comparing him to a portrait in her father's gallery. Two ring-doves appear; an archery wager follows in which Jemshid shoots the female dove, pinning its wings, and the male returns to it. A nurse confirms by horoscope and recognition that the stranger is Jemshid and foretells the princess will bear a son who will conquer the world. A portrait is brought and matches; Jemshid recognizes himself in it and weeps, remembering his lost diadem and throne.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The princess and the youth sit near a fountain on royal carpets; music and wine are called for.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Handmaids wash the stranger's feet and attend by the lake margin.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The youth drinks three cups of wine, which is said to banish sorrow and memory of affliction; he asks for more wine and speaks in praise of wine's effects.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The princess conjectures the youth is King Jemshid and remembers a portrait of Jemshid in her father's gallery to compare features, then decides it is unnecessary.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "A male and female ring-dove alight on the wall and coo; the princess calls for bow and arrows and asks Jemshid which bird she should hit; he takes the bow and proposes a wager tied to the female dove being hit.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Jemshid shoots the female dove so that both wings are transfixed and pinned together; the male flies away and then returns to the same spot.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The bow is described as so strong that no warrior in the kingdom can draw its string; the princess interprets the stranger's ease with it as proof he is King Jemshid, son of Tahmers, called the Binder of Demons.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "The nurse appears, examines Jemshid, recognizes him, and says her horoscope foretold that the princess will have Jemshid as spouse and be blessed with a son who will conquer the world.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "A portrait is brought; the princess finds the resemblance exact and gives it to Jemshid; he recognizes his likeness, remembers his former diadem and throne, and weeps.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    }
