  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "In a courtship episode, Mihrb, chief of Kabul and descendant of Zohak’s line, receives Zal (Zal/Zl), son of Sam. Zal learns of Mihrb’s secluded daughter Rudabeh and becomes enamored. Mihrb invites Zal to a banquet, but Zal refuses for fear that his father Sam and the King of Persia would be offended by ties to Zohak’s family. Back in Kabul, Mihrb’s praise of Zal prompts Rudabeh to confess her love to her attendants; they initially object, citing Zal’s strange upbringing with the Simurgh and his white hair, but she persists. The attendants then act to bring the lovers together: they go rose-gathering by a clear stream near Zal’s tent; after Zal shoots a bird and approaches, they learn his identity and speak of a possible union between Zal and Rudabeh. Zal responds by sending gifts, including two finger-rings secretly to Rudabeh.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Mihrb, chief of Kabul, is said to be descended from the family of Zohak and pays annual tribute to Sam to secure the safety of his state.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Mihrb goes out of the city to meet Zal and is hospitably entertained by him.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Zal discovers that Mihrb has a daughter, Rudabeh, who is screened from public view and described as exceptionally beautiful.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The description of Rudabeh causes Zal to feel violent affection, and his imagination adds to her charms.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Mihrb invites Zal to a banquet, but Zal refuses because accepting an invitation from a relation of Zohak would offend Sam and the King of Persia.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "In Mihrb’s harem, his wife Sindokht asks about Zal, including what he says about his sojourn with the Simurgh.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Mihrb praises Zal as valiant, accomplished, and handsome, with the only defect being white hair; Rudabeh hears this and falls into admiration and love.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Rudabeh privately confesses to her attendants that she loves Zal and thinks of him day and night and sees him in her dreams, asking for their aid.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "The attendants remonstrate against Rudabeh’s attachment, calling Zal a strange being and referring to him as a nursling of a mountain bird and noting his hoary locks.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Rudabeh insists her attachment is fixed; she rejects other rulers and wealth and says she worships Zal alone and cannot part from his image.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "In springtime, the attendants go toward Zal’s halting-place near the city, appearing to gather roses along the banks of a clear stream opposite Zal’s tent.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Zal shoots a bird rising from the water with an arrow; as he approaches the rose-gatherers, they learn from his servant that he is Nam-ruz, son of Sam, also called Dustan; the damsels speak of Rudabeh and propose that if Zal and Rudabeh are of equal rank their union may be effected, after which Zal prepares presents including two secret finger-rings for Rudabeh.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11",
        "ev:12"
      ]
    }
