  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "A crafty king selects troops and instructs his chiefs to keep secret a plan to set a young warrior against his father Rustem, so that father and son will fight as enemies. The king sends chiefs with lavish gifts (crown, jeweled throne, horses, camels) and a letter offering aid and kingship. The youth accepts, marches toward Persia with drums and a large army, and spreads destruction. At a barrier-fort, a Persian defender (Hujr) rides out alone and challenges the invader; insults are exchanged. In the duel, Sohrb’s spear strikes Hujr, throws him to the ground, and Sohrb stands over him with a dagger but spares his life; Hujr is bound as a captive and sent to one of the chiefs.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
