  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Rustem speaks privately about the coming duel and gives instructions in case he dies. At dawn Sohrab doubts whether his opponent is Rustem (his father) and considers refraining, but Humn dismisses the idea. Sohrab proposes friendship and asks the Champion to reveal his name; Rustem refuses and insists on combat. They wrestle; Sohrab throws Rustem and is about to kill him, but Rustem claims a rule that a chief may be twice overthrown, so Sohrab releases him. Humn reproaches Sohrab for letting Rustem go; Sohrab says the next day's contest will decide Rustem's fate.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Rustem (called 'the Champion') anticipates a duel with a 'valiant Turk' and instructs a companion to assemble warriors at dawn; if Rustem wins they should share the triumph, and if he dies they should be guided home and his parents informed and comforted.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Rustem states that death comes for all and names earlier heroes (Kirshsp, Sm, Narmn, Jemshd) as examples who could not escape fate, concluding that Rustem too must fall.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "At dawn the warriors arm; Sohrab observes his opponent and says his 'filial' feelings are stirred and that his mother's tokens and other proofs make him think the opponent is Rustem, making him fear provoking his father's rage and bringing shame.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Humn answers Sohrab that the thought is vain; he says he has seen Rustem fight and that while the horse resembles Rustem's, it is not as strong or beautiful.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Sohrab proposes that they sit together, seek forgiveness, bind friendship, feast, and asks the Champion to state his name, directly asking whether he is Rustem.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Rustem rejects Sohrab's proposal as a delaying stratagem, insists on the promised struggle, and Sohrab appeals to Heaven as they prepare to fight.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Both combatants bind their horses to a neighboring rock and wrestle fiercely; Sohrab seizes Rustem by the girdle, throws him, and bestrides him with sword raised to behead him.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Rustem stops Sohrab by claiming a law that a chief may fight until twice overthrown, and that only after the second fall may his blood be spilled; Sohrab believes him and releases him.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Humn laments that Sohrab has freed Rustem and warns him never again to suspend the final blow or trust a foe's seeming weakness; Sohrab dismisses the complaint and says tomorrow's contest will decide Rustem's fate.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
