  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "A prognostication warns Afrsiyab that fighting Saiwush will lead to defeat and ruin; Afrsiyab seeks peace by sending gifts and an embassy. A treaty is negotiated with hostages and territorial restoration, but Kai-Kus rejects it and orders renewed war, commanding Saiwush to return with the hostages. Offended and bound by his pledged word, Saiwush chooses exile and crosses the Jihun to seek Afrsiyab’s protection, recounting earlier injustice and a past trial by fire that proved his innocence. Afrsiyab receives Saiwush with honors, scattering gold and incense and welcoming him publicly.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "A prediction based on a horoscope says that if Afrsiyab rushes to conflict against Saiwush, the Turkish cohorts will be slain; if Saiwush dies, Afrsiyab’s country will also lose throne and diadem.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Afrsiyab accepts the interpretation and avoids war; he sends Gerswaz to Saiwush’s headquarters with splendid presents and a written dispatch proposing an end to hostilities.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "A council decides to demand one hundred distinguished heroes as hostages and restoration of provinces taken from Iran; Afrsiyab approves, sends a hundred warriors, and delivers territories to Saiwush.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Afrsiyab says he has had a terrible dream and will surrender what is required rather than go to war.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Rumor reports Afrsiyab’s dream to Kai-Kus; Kai-Kus’s astrologers prognosticate the certain ruin of the Turanian king, but Rustem argues the treaty terms are good and incurs Kai-Kus’s displeasure.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Kai-Kus appoints Tus leader of the Persian army, orders him to march against Afrsiyab, and commands Saiwush to return with the hundred hostages.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Saiwush says he has pledged his word to fulfill the terms and worries about keeping faith; his chiefs advise writing again and being ready to renew war and return the hostages.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Saiwush resolves to abandon his country and join Afrsiyab; he writes that Kai-Kus refused to ratify the peace and that he must return hostages and seek protection in Turan.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Afrasiyab replies that peace was settled with Saiwush, not with his father; he offers Saiwush a peaceful independent province or support for war, stating that with Rustem Persia would be easy to conquer.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Saiwush entrusts Balkh, the army, and treasure to Bhram to be delivered to Tus; he takes three hundred chosen horsemen and crosses the Jihun toward Afrsiyab’s court.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Saiwush writes to Kai-Kus recounting earlier wrongs, including Sudaveh’s treachery and that he was forced to brave devouring fire to prove innocence; he says God delivered him, and he laments his father’s anger despite gaining spoils and a hundred hostages guaranteeing peace.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Afrasiyab warmly receives Saiwush; from the city gates to the palace, gold and incense are scattered over Saiwush’s head in a customary manner and public welcomes are uttered.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    }
