  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Afrasiyab orders ferrymen on the Jihun to stop three fugitives. When the ferrymen demand extravagant payment, Kai-khosru, his mother Ferangs, and Gw ride their horses through the dangerous river and reach the far shore. Afrasiyab arrives too late to catch them and is dissuaded from crossing. After entering Persian territory, Kai-khosru is welcomed and symbolically enthroned; nobles pledge obedience, but Ts refuses and supports Frburz as heir. Gdarz gathers forces to compel compliance, while Ts reflects that he would rather avoid civil war and fight foreign enemies instead.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Afrasiyab sends instructions to all ferrymen of the Jihun, describing three travelers and ordering that they be prevented from crossing, while he pursues with an army moving rapidly night and day.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "On the riverbank, Gw asks ferrymen to show certificates; they claim the certificates are lost and demand as fare (in turn) Gw's black horse, then the accompanying 'damsel' (whom Gw identifies as the youth's mother), then the youth's crown, then Gw's armor, refusing money and telling them to cross as they can if they will not yield these items.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Gw urges immediate action and cites Kavah the blacksmith rescuing Feridn by crossing a stream in armor; under an auspicious omen and trust in God's protection, Kai-khosru, Ferangs, and Gw ride their horses into the rapid river and successfully reach the opposite shore, astonishing the ferrymen.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "As they touch the shore, Afrsiyb arrives with his army and sees the fugitives beyond reach; he orders the ferrymen to ready boats, but Hmn argues only a few troops can cross and they would face a large enemy force, so Afrsiyb turns back to Trn.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "After crossing into the Persian empire, Gw gives thanks to God and sends news to Ks; Ks rejoices, sends an honorary deputation led by Gdarz, greets the young prince with tears and kisses, provides a throne like his own, seats Kai-khosru on it, and commands nobles and warriors to obey him.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "All pledge allegiance except Ts, who leaves in disgust, tells Frburz he will obey him as heir rather than the 'infant' brought from a desert, refuses a public banquet invitation, and repeats his refusal when Gw is sent to invite him again.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Gw reports Ts's resistance; Gdarz becomes angry and marches with twelve thousand men and seventy-eight kinsmen (and Gw) to support Kai-khosru by force; Ts prepares to meet them but internally reasons against initiating civil war, preferring conflict only against foreign power ('Tartar foe').",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    }
  ],
