    "summary": "At the Persian court, Tus (Ts) and the hero Gudarz (Gdarz) quarrel over succession and loyalty: Tus states he will serve Kai-Kaus (Ks) but would owe allegiance to Farburz (Frburz) if Kai-Kaus abdicates; Gudarz argues that the murdered Siyavush (Saiwush) should be avenged/appeased by enthroning Kai-Khosru (Khosru), likening him to Feridun (Feridn). Their dispute turns into an exchange of insults, including Gudarz’s defense of his descent from the blacksmith Kavah, who once opposed Zahhak (Zohk) and his serpents. The king stops the quarrel and proposes a trial to decide the successor: both princes will attempt to conquer a nearby demon-fortress called Bahmen, from which continuous fire issues. Farburz goes first and fails, with many soldiers dying from the heat and no entrance found. Khosru then approaches, recognizes sorcery, and uses knowledge from a heavenly dream: he writes the name of God and binds it to his javelin, raises it, and with prayer the fires die and darkness falls; his troops shoot arrows, slaying many demons. Light returns; surviving fiends flee; the gate becomes visible; the fortress is sacked. Khosru erects a temple to commemorate the victory and returns triumphantly, pleasing the king.",
