  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Manjeh sends her nurse to question a handsome youth beneath a cypress tree. The youth, Byzun, identifies himself as a Persian noble on a boar-hunting mission and offers the nurse gems and a gold coronet to arrange an introduction. Manjeh welcomes him into her tent; they feast for three days. When he must depart, she has a narcotic given to him and has him secretly carried in disguise into Turan, to Afrsiyb’s palace, where he awakens fearful of witchery but remains under Manjeh’s persuasion. The affair is discovered; Afrsiyb rages and delivers a bitter speech condemning daughters and women.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Manjeh instructs her nurse to ask the radiant youth beneath a cypress tree for his name and to convey her admiration and desire to know his feelings.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Byzun replies that he is not Saiwush; he is the son of Gw and has come from Irn with the king’s permission to kill wild boars, sending their heads and tusks to Kai-khosru.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Byzun says he forgot home and father after hearing of Manjeh’s beauty and asks the nurse to introduce him, offering gems and a gold coronet as gifts.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Manjeh promptly consents and invites the youth to make her summer-tent his home, declaring her devotion in verse.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Byzun goes unobserved to Manjeh’s tent; she embraces him, removes his Kaini girdle for comfort, and asks him to recount the boar-hunt.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Byzun is perfumed with musk and rose-water; refreshments, music, attendants, ornate decorations, and wine in crystal goblets are described; the joyful feast lasts three nights and three days.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "On the fourth day, Manjeh orders a narcotic draught; while Byzun sleeps insensible he is conveyed secretly and in disguise into Trn and taken to Afrsiyb’s palace, where he later awakes.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Byzun, fearing betrayal and a fatal outcome and suspecting witchery, attempts to leave seclusion but stays due to Manjeh’s blandishments and continued enjoyment.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Manjeh urges Byzun to be glad and to drink wine, contrasting a time for war with a time for soothing life’s ills.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The love of the princess for a Persian youth is discovered; guards fear punishment; Afrsiyb rages and delivers a hostile speech about daughters and women.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    }
