  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "A king of Rum conquers Khuz after his champion Gushtasp defeats the ruler Als in single combat. Gushtasp then urges an invasion of Iran; the king of Rum sends threats to Lohursp. Lohursp laughs, learns from the messenger that the champion is named Gushtasp and infers he is his own son, and replies with defiance. Lohursp sends Zarr as ambassador and as intermediary to ask Gushtasp forgiveness and invite him home to receive the crown, as Lohursp wishes to withdraw into prayer like Kai-khosru. Zarr meets Gushtasp, who returns with his wife Kitabn; Lohursp welcomes him, seats him beside himself on a golden royal chair, and finally gives him the crown.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "A king of Rum demands that Als, ruler of Khuz, surrender his kingdom or prepare for war; armies assemble and battle ensues.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Gushtasp challenges Als to single combat, defeats him, drags him in fetters before the king of Rum, and the city and kingdom of Khuz are taken and plundered.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "After the victory, Gushtasp proposes invading Iran and says he can fight Lohursp even with a small army; the king of Rum sends Lohursp a letter demanding half the kingdom or threatening total dispossession.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Lohursp laughs at the threat and asks how Khuz was captured and how Als was killed; the messenger attributes success to a youth who first killed a wolf, then a dragon, and then dragged Als from his horse.",
      "category": "sequence",
