    "summary": "A devastated landscape and a battle are described. Zerdusht advises King Gushtsp to consult his vizir Jamsp, who reads the king’s horoscope and predicts heavy losses among Gushtsp’s kin but ultimate victory. In the ensuing conflict several warriors die, including Zarr, who is killed by the demon Bai-derafsh. Gushtsp laments and promises his son Isfendiyr the crown and throne if he defeats the demon and enemy; he also gives Isfendiyr a famed black horse, Behzd. Isfendiyr kills Bai-derafsh, takes the severed head, and the enemy king Arjsp retreats; many enemies surrender and are taken prisoner. Gushtsp mourns Zarr, places him in a coffin, builds a lofty monument, and maintains ever-burning lights there; he teaches fire worship and seeks to establish Zerdusht’s religion. A casualty count is given, victory is celebrated, and Gushtsp crowns Isfendiyr, who appears holding a cow-headed mace.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
