  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "The passage introduces Isfendiyr’s “seven labours” (Heft-khan) in parallel to Rustem’s. Isfendiyr questions Kurugsar about routes to a brazen fortress: a pleasant three-month road, a harsher two-month road, or a seven-day route called the Heft-khan where monsters and magical dangers appear at each stage. Isfendiyr chooses the shortest perilous route, trusting God and fate. After a banquet with heavy wine, Kurugsar still urges the safer path and refuses to accompany Isfendiyr on the Heft-khan. Suspecting treachery, Isfendiyr chains Kurugsar, claiming it is to keep him from fleeing and that the bonds act as a protective charm. Isfendiyr then receives Gushtsp’s blessing and departs with twelve thousand horsemen and treasure to free his sisters and conquer Arjsp’s kingdom.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
