  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Gushtsp orders his son Isfendiyr, named successor, to undertake a war-expedition to Sistan. Isfendiyr agrees after Jamsp urges obedience to a father's command, despite his mother Kitabn warning that Rustem cannot be bound and that the war will bring no honor. Isfendiyr marches with a large army and treasure; an incident with a camel is taken as a bad omen, but he continues. Near Sistan, Isfendiyr sends his son Bahman to Rustem requesting a welcoming deputation; Zl counsels Rustem to receive the prince. Rustem welcomes Isfendiyr and invites him to his house, but Isfendiyr insists he cannot delay and instead demands that Rustem allow himself to be bound in chains and taken to Gushtsp. Rustem asks first for guest-right. They debate the honor-implications of eating 'bread and salt' and fighting afterward, and they agree to drink wine together (each his own). Rustem then consults Zl and praises Isfendiyr's qualities, attributing them to Feridun.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
