  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Bashtan urges Isfendiyr to secure Rustem, but Rustem returns and presses Isfendiyr to accept hospitality. A dispute follows: Rustem recites his achievements and objects to being seated on Isfendiyr's left, taking the right-hand place instead. Isfendiyr insults Rustem by recounting a degrading story about Rustem's father Zal being cast out and raised by the Simurgh; Rustem counters with genealogy, royal lineage, and a catalog of conquests and rescues. Isfendiyr replies by asserting his own superior exploits (Heft-khan and Brazen Fortress) and claims to have spread the blessings of his heaven-derived fire-worshipping religion as both sovereign and prophet. Rustem answers that Isfendiyr had a large armed force, while Rustem relied mainly on his horse Rakush and sword, and he describes rescuing Kay Kavus and slaying monsters in caves.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Bashtan reproaches Isfendiyr for letting an enemy go and suggests that subduing Rustem cannot be done easily by force.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Rustem appears again, invites Isfendiyr to become his guest, and says he is sent by his father to offer hospitality.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Isfendiyr refuses to go farther and tells Rustem to accompany him to Iran.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Rustem enumerates his achievements and speaks of fettering the emperor of Chin, breaking the enchantment of the Seven Khans, guarding Persian kings, and clearing the world of foes.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Isfendiyr is angered, considers throwing a dagger, but instead seats Rustem on his left; Rustem refuses and seats himself on Isfendiyr's right without permission.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Isfendiyr claims he has heard that Zal was of demon extraction, was cast into the desert, and was raised by the Simurgh and fed carrion until taken back and taught human manners and science.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Rustem denies the insult, asserts shared origin and princely descent, and lists having refused kingship and having conquered or subdued multiple kings and foes, including Afrasiyab, the king of Hamavaran, the Khakan of Chin, demons of Mazandaran, the White Demon, and Akwan Div.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Isfendiyr claims his Heft-khan and capture of the Brazen Fortress surpass Rustem's, and says he diffused the blessings of his heaven-derived fire-worshipping religion as a sovereign and prophet.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Rustem replies that Isfendiyr had twelve thousand armed men, while Rustem had only Rakush and his sword, and he contrasts releasing two sisters with rescuing Kay Kavus and slaying monsters roaring in caves.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    }
  ],
