  },
  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Failaks crowns Sikander and appoints Arist as vizier, urging virtue, humility, justice, and receptiveness to counsel. After Sikander becomes king, Dr demands customary tribute; Sikander refuses and threatens invasion. Before battle, Sikander enters Dr's camp disguised as an envoy, converses with Dr, and at a feast keeps four cups given to him, claiming it is his country's custom not to return a cup once placed in an ambassador's hands. When recognized, he escapes on horseback with the cups. He shows the cups to his own court as proof of his daring and as confirmation of the enemy's condition. After extended fighting, Dr is forced to flee and is pursued to the Euphrates; Sikander takes the capital, defeats Dr again, and then wins Persian loyalty by conciliation and by claiming descent from Drb and promising just, paternal care.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Before his death, Failaks places the crown of sovereignty on Sikander's head and appoints Arist (a disciple of Afltn) as vizier; Failaks counsels Sikander to pursue virtue, avoid vanity and pride, and be just and merciful.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "After Sikander's elevation, Dr sends an envoy to claim customary tribute; Sikander replies that Persia should now pay tribute to Rm and threatens invasion and wider conquest if refused.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Sikander enters the Persian camp in the assumed character of an envoy to learn the enemy's condition; Dr questions him, and the envoy claims Sikander seeks travel and inspection and asks only for free passage, though ready for war.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Dr orders refreshments and wine; the envoy drinks and does not return the goblet, saying it is the custom in his country not to receive back a cup once given to an ambassador; he keeps four cups in succession.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "During the feast, someone recognizes Sikander; he escapes the tent, taking the four cups, mounts his horse, and rides away; pursuit is hindered by night.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Sikander shows the four golden cups to Aristtals and courtiers and says he took them from the enemy's table and has ascertained Dr's strength and numbers; preparations for battle follow.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The armies fight seven days without a decisive blow; on the eighth Dr is compelled to flee and is pursued with slaughter to the banks of the Euphrates; Sikander returns to take the capital, defeats Dr again after Dr regroups, then conciliates the people and claims descent from Drb, prompting legions to join him.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
