  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Sikander demands submission from Kaid of Ind and requires four exceptional possessions (a daughter, a minister, a physician, and a valuable goblet); he marries Kaid's daughter and drinks from the goblet, after which Kaid personally acknowledges Sikander's dominion. Sikander then wars with Far of Kanj, whose elephants terrify Sikander's troops; counsellors devise iron horse-and-rider engines filled with combustibles that are ignited to explode and burn the elephants, leading to Far's defeat and death. After further travels (including to Mekka), Sikander visits two wish-granting trees (male by day, female by night) whose voice is interpreted by a priest as a prophecy limiting Sikander's remaining life and foretelling his death at Kashan. In another city he is told of the giants/demons Yjuj and Mjuj (Gog and Magog) who prey on people; Sikander builds a massive barrier that they cannot scale. Later he comes to a place with many inhabitants and a lofty mountain; at its foot he kills a guardian-dragon.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Kaid of Ind says he will submit but claims to possess four unique things: a daughter, a wise minister, a skilful physician, and a goblet of inestimable value.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Sikander demands these four things; Kaid complies; Sikander holds a feast, marries Kaid's daughter, and drinks wine from the goblet taken from her hands; Kaid then personally acknowledges Sikander's authority.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Far, king of Kanj, refuses to submit and taunts Sikander as a Greek; Sikander is angered.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Sikander's force is stated as eighty thousand men (Irnians, Rms, and Inds), while Far has sixty thousand horsemen and two thousand elephants; Sikander's troops are terrified by the elephants.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Aristtals and other counsellors propose iron horse-and-rider figures on wheels filled with combustibles, ignited by a soldier using a long staff to cause an explosion and blaze; Sikander orders a thousand machines made by blacksmiths and artisans.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "When the machines are fired, many elephants are burned and destroyed; the rest and the troops flee; Sikander later slays Far and becomes ruler of Kanj.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "After conquering Kanj, Sikander goes to Mekka with rich presents and offerings and then travels to other cities where he is received with homage.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "People tell Sikander of two trees (male and female) that speak (male by day, female by night) and that those with wishes go there to have desires accomplished; Sikander goes to them and hears a terrible sound.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "A priest interprets the sound as meaning Sikander has fourteen years left; the trees (or voice) answer that Sikander will die at Kashan, and Sikander weeps and leaves in sorrow.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Inhabitants of another city report being attacked at night by two demons/giants who devour men and goats; they name them Yjuj and Mjuj (Gog and Magog); Sikander orders a barrier built 500 yards high and 300 yards wide that the giants cannot scale.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Sikander later reaches a place with countless inhabitants and sees a lofty mountain; at the mountain's foot he finds a guardian-dragon and kills it.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    }
  ],
