    "summary": "A sequence of editorial footnotes explaining terms and episodes from Persian epic/romance material, including: heraldic standards and family arms; fatalism and destiny in the Rustem–Sohrb story; a classical parallel (Themistocles’ tapestry metaphor); large royal families; military center terminology; Ahirmun as a demon/principle of evil; a girdle as a royal gift/token (compared to Jonathan and David); crocodile imagery in war; heroes’ cunning; a weapon/term (Umud) meaning iron implement/column; proverbial advice on not underestimating an enemy; Rustem’s piety and attribution of victory to God; verses about destiny thirsting for blood (attributed to Unsar and possibly later incorporated); Jemshid as a recurring poetic exemplar; medicated draughts and claims of drugs to recover the dead; a troop crossing of the Jihn river; Manjeh’s lineage; musical entertainment parallels (Theocritus/Adonis and Arabian Nights); and a note on Alexander’s deathplace traditions.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
