    "summary": "An editorial introduction to Edward FitzGerald’s English translation of Omar Khayyam’s Rubaiyat. It discusses the work’s form (a series of independent quatrains), recurring sensual imagery (roses, nightingale, wine, love), and its dominant tone of melancholy, misanthropy, despair, and fear of death and Fate. It characterizes Omar as a great mathematician whose analytic mind shapes the poem’s finish and wit, and it frames the poems as a revolt against religious bigotry while also showing traces of mysticism, longing for knowledge of the invisible, and foreboding that the grave is life’s end. The introduction compares Omar’s outlook to modern European intellectual pessimism and notes the popularity of the translation with English readers.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
