  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "A lover addresses a beloved whose beauty has captivated the lover’s heart and from whom no one can escape. The speaker describes sighing, being pierced by a metaphorical dart, and suffering inner ills. The speaker refrains from likening the beloved’s lips to rubies when vivid words are scorned by an unrefined soul; the beloved’s beauty grows daily beyond comparison even to the night-star. The speaker offers heart and soul, dismissing any \"envoy of grief\" as unnecessary. The poem ends by addressing Hafiz: having entered Love’s path, one should grasp \"his\" skirt and sever all ties from that day forward.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
