    "summary": "A devotional poem (CLXXX) addresses an unlearned seeker, insisting that one who has not traveled love’s path cannot guide others. The seeker is urged to remain in the school of Truth learning from “Master Love,” to practice abstinence (sleep and food), to cleanse oneself of worldly “dross,” and to undergo inner transformation likened to alchemy (transmuting to gold). The poem describes divine light shining on the one who effaces self on the path, and uses the image of plunging into God’s sea—so vast that even the Seven Seas cannot wet a hair. It closes with Hafiz advising that to desire union one must become dust at the threshold of the keen-sighted. After the poem, a section of footnotes to the Divan provides explanatory notes on various terms and allusions (e.g., Magian/shaikh, Jemshid, Sikander/Alexander, Korah and Moses, Noah, Rizwan, lote-tree, etc.).",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
