  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Asaf (Solomon’s vizier) guarded the imperial signet ring, described as having magical properties, and it was stolen while in his care.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Solomon is described as granting audiences to animals and insects; an ant brings a blade of grass as an offering and rebukes Asaf for careless guardianship of the royal treasure.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The notes state that in the poem Asaf symbolizes the poet’s friend/favorite, the ant implies a small facial hair, and the lost signet of Jam implies a very small, delicate mouth.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Majnun is identified as a celebrated lover driven mad by the charms of Layla.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "A footnote explains a tavern/shrine image: other revelers have preceded the speaker, and their heads are now clay in a potter’s field of the earth.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "A Shiraz wild tulip has a dark central spot compared to a pre-ordained ‘brand of love’ imprinted on Hafiz’s heart on the ‘Past Day of Eternity.’",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Khosru (Cyrus) is described as a royal title used in the plural for monarchs in general; ‘kiblah’ is defined as the direction/point toward which worshippers turn in prayer.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Korah is described as disobeying Moses and being swallowed up by the earth with his treasures, and said to be still sinking deeper.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:6"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Solomon’s glories are called vain; Asaf was his minister, the East wind his courser, and knowledge of the language of birds an accomplishment, but time swept them away.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:7"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The ‘Comment of the Comments’ is identified as a celebrated explanatory treatise on the Koran.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:8"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Qaf is described as a fabulous mountain encircling the world; the poet is said to ridicule ascetics in relation to this image.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:9"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "False coiners are described as inferior poets trying to pass off their works as Hafiz’s.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:10"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Aiman (Happiness) is identified as the valley where God appeared to Moses; metaphorically, it is the abode of the Beloved.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:11"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "Mihrab is defined as the niche in a mosque toward which Muslims pray.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:12"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "Kalandars are described as a dervish order that wanders and begs; the note contrasts ‘worthless sectaries’ who shave beard and tonsure with a ‘true/spiritual’ kalandar who follows duty scrupulously.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:13"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "Zoroaster is identified as a prophet of fire-worshippers; Nimrod is said to have practiced a similar religion.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:14"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "‘Ad and Thamud are described as Arab tribes exterminated by God for disobeying the prophet Salih.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:15"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:18",
      "text": "A ‘grain’ is explained as a grain of wheat, identified in Islamic tradition as the forbidden fruit of Paradise.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:16"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:19",
      "text": "Kamal is described as an Arab whose glance inflicted death.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:17"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:20",
      "text": "‘Alif-form’ is explained as an upright, straight form, like the letter alif; ‘men who glance’ are lovers, and the true/spiritual lover is said to be one who loves God.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:18"]
    }
